Now that was a fun ride.

No screenshots or anything, I don't think they're necessary for this
one.  Helje, who went through... well, a LOT, finally gets to the
Astral Plane, flips the bird to the mosh pits there, and drops the
Amulet on Altar Number 2.  Second time to the Astral Plane, first
ascension.  My high score list will never be the same. *grin*

Now for the fun (read: long) part - reliving the interesting stuff!

Helje was slightly lucky in the beginning parts of the game, finding
an altar on the second floor of the dungeon and getting lucky enough
to convert it with a few tries.  After a little dungeon exploration, I
tromped back a few levels, appeased Tyr with whatever I could grab,
and was awarded for my dungeon-cleaning efforts with a Big Smashy
Hammer called Mjollnir.  Having this weapon at level 5 is a good
thing.  (Can't have too many good things, so I converted her longsword
to Excalibur later on - not that it was used much.)

After finding a leocrotta and some other nasties down further, I
decided carefully (panicked and ran like heck) to head for Minetown to
pick up what she could find there.  A co-aligned altar at Minetown was
awfully helpful for holy water and lighting purposes.  Remembering the
need for candles way way way later on, I bought up those... but only
had six.  Well, here's hoping I find some later.  Aside from that, the
Mines were an impressive boon - on top of the luckstone and some elven
mithril armor, found an old pile of monk bones, complete with a +4
robe and a touchstone, both of which I promptly blessed. (Genjo the
monk got offed by a leocrotta, which I took care of this time with a
conveniently found wand of magic missile.)

Armored down into the negatives, the leocrotta and friends down in the
Dungeons proper were only a minor trouble.  Sokoban yielded the amulet
of reflection - which I ditched not two levels later in favor of a
silver shield.  After that, things proceeded with little trouble - the
Quest and the next fifteen-odd levels of dungeon went by smoothly. 
Except for a few frustrating caveats...

No bag of holding, no gauntlets of power, very little Gehennom-worthy
armor.  The oilskin cloak I nicked off a troll and a silver shield
were the only bit of armor that followed me all the way through.

But there were some fun parts too... during the Quest, witnessed this:
 "The fire giant puts on an amulet of life saving."  Grr... that's MY
amulet.  But how the heck am I going to get it off of him?

Hey look, wand of polymorph.  Problem solved.  Zap - orc zombie!  No
life to save, no amulet lost.  Grabbed that sucker up and wondered
just how cool I am.

Without any means of crossing water, I had to dig down into the deeper
areas and hope I found something - or hope that I could wish some up. 
By the time I cleared the Castle, I still had no gauntlets of power -
even after some attempts at polypiling in the armory - still no bag of
holding, and not even any dragon scales.  I used the wand for the bag,
the gauntlets, and the armor - and after the recharge, I only had 2
wishes left (including the wand-burner).  Crap.  I need more than
this... I decided to head up for Medusa, finish her off, and see if I
could grab levitation boots while there.  Levitation boots were there,
thank goodness, but I could still use a lot more stuff for Gehennom...

Oh hey, look!  Right outside Medusa's building, a wand of wishing, 3
charges.  Time to splurge!

A magic marker, some scrolls of genocide (they didn't naturally pop up
in the game at all), a ring o' teleport control, some speed boots
later, I sorta lost count of how many I had left.  But that was all I
needed - and damned if I was going to burn the last one.  I needed
that for the endgame.

Valley of the Dead and Gehennom zipped past quickly.  Notable finds
were another magic marker (whee!), a magic lamp (YAY!), a -2 helm of
brilliance which I did not hesitate to enchant to +5 though I didn't
really need it, Vlad's life-saver amulet, another magic lamp (getting
kinda silly now, but yay!) and a wish on Vlad's throne (for a wand of
death - the Wizard can kiss my mighty warrioress rear.) Snagged Orcus'
wand with two charges left, and grabbed a third wand of death from a
random soldier who had the common sense to zap at a person with a
silver shield.

Moloch's Sanctum was fun - the Wizard jumped me before I reached the
main room, and in the ensuing madness Yeenoghu wandered in.  Had to
slaughter 'em both.  The Wiz helpfully called up a cockatrice - why
thank you, caddy, I believe I'll be using my chicken wedge today - so
the rest of the Sanctum got real decorative afterwards.  But the High
Priest didn't have the amulet.

After briefly panicking, I went back to the wizard - who had one. 
Grabbed it up, and waited for a moment to see if it gave me the
thumbs-up... and clairvoyance showed me the way.  Marked it "THE REAL
ONE" and started the mad dash.

Moloch likes to screw with lawful characters trying to get out of
Gehennom.  Turd.  Flipped the Wiz the wand twice before getting out of
there, and after that the trip was a breeze.  Earth plane was no
problem at all.  Air plane threatened to kill me, but didn't make it. 
Fire plane was like a touchdown run - there's the goal, dodge the
elementals, gah, tackled, but he saves and touchdown!

Water plane could have been frustrating... but the Orb of Fate fixes
that problem real nice.

Astral Plane was easier than expected.  With a wand of death in one
hand and a wand of teleportation in the other, Helje sweeped through
two mosh pits without much trouble.  But the teleporter accidentally
whisked away the high priest on the second altar... paused for a
moment.  Okay, you've got a 50-50 chance of this being the right one. 
Do you take it?  Do you dash for the other one?  I've gotta be sure...

In doubt, I prayed at the unknown altar...  Tyr is well-pleased, and
blesses all my stuff.  Hi Tyr, good to see you're okay.  Brought you a
present, hope you like it.

Notable ending equipment:
Amulet of life saving - I lost the first one because I fell into a pit
swinging my chicken wedge around in the Sanctum.  Tossed it away after
that.  Aside from that dumb mistake, never died.
Orb of Fate - GOD this thing is useful in the Planes.
Two magic lamps and a wand of wishing (1:0) - used the last wish on
that wand for a blessed scroll of charging in the Astral Plane for a
worn-out wand of death.  Never used the lamps - well, used one for
light.  But... darn, three wishes unspent.  I could have used those.
Eight, count'em, eight blessed potions of full healing - Argh!  I was
saving EVERY SINGLE ONE I FOUND for the Astral Plane!  And I never
used one of them!
Every single valuable gem in the Mines, the Dungeon, the Quest, and
Gehennom - What can I say, I wanted a good top score.

So with 306 max hp, and I don't even remember how many points, Helje
hits number one at the top score list, so I'll see it whenever I play
(and die) again - Helje (Val-Dwa-Fem-Law) ascended to
demigoddess-hood.

Now I've got something to really be proud of.

